# Rollup

## Description

Groups input by an optional key, runs a per-group CTL2 lifecycle (`initGroup` → `updateGroup` per record → `finishGroup` → `transform`), and emits one or more records per group to one or more output ports. More expressive than AGGREGATE — the right choice when group reduction is custom (not just SUM/COUNT/AVG) or when you need multi-port routing per group.

## Purpose & when to use

Reach for ROLLUP when:
- Per-group reduction is custom (collect into a list, build a JSON, conditional accumulation).
- You need multiple output records per group, possibly to different ports.
- You're "exploding" multi-value fields into normalized child records.

Don't use ROLLUP when:
- Standard SUM/COUNT/AVG/MIN/MAX is enough → use AGGREGATE (declarative, no CTL).
- You need straightforward N:1 collapsing → use DENORMALIZER.

## XML template

```xml
<Node id="ROLLUP0" type="ROLLUP"
      groupKeyFields="customer_id"
      groupAccumulatorMetadataId="META_ACC"
      inputSorted="true"
      equalNULL="true"
      guiX="500" guiY="200">
  <attr name="transform"><![CDATA[//#CTL2 — see ## CTL2 contract]]></attr>
</Node>
```

- `type="ROLLUP"`.
- One of `transform` / `transformUrl` / `transformClassName` is **required** (the CTL2 body is anchored in the `transform` `<attr>` CDATA).
- Optional attributes: `groupKeyFields`, `groupAccumulatorMetadataId`, `inputSorted`, `equalNULL`.
- The per-group accumulator (when used) needs its own `<Metadata>` block, referenced by `groupAccumulatorMetadataId`:

```xml
<Metadata id="META_ACC">
  <Record name="OrderAcc" type="delimited">
    <Field name="count"        type="integer"/>
    <Field name="totalFreight" type="decimal"/>
  </Record>
</Metadata>
```

## Properties

| Property | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `groupKeyFields` | — | Semicolon-separated input field names. If omitted, all records form one group. |
| `groupAccumulatorMetadataId` | — | Metadata ID for the accumulator record (per-group state). If omitted, `VoidMetadata` is used and no state can be carried across `updateGroup` calls. |
| `transform` / `transformUrl` / `transformClassName` | required | One of these. Inline CTL2 (or external file / Java class). |
| `transformUrlCharset` | — | Charset of the external `.ctl` referenced by `transformUrl`. |
| `inputSorted` | `true` | Default assumes input is sorted by `groupKeyFields`. Set `false` for unsorted input (uses an internal hash). |
| `equalNULL` | `true` | Null key values treated as equal (same group). |

## Input & output metadata

One required input; outputs 1–N, **each can have different metadata**. Nothing is auto-propagated — define each output `<Metadata>` yourself; the transform functions populate them. The optional group-accumulator metadata (via `groupAccumulatorMetadataId`) persists across `updateGroup` calls within a group.

```
auto_propagated_metadata: none   # you must define each output <Metadata> AND populate it in the transform functions (CTL2)
```

**Precondition — sorted input:** with `inputSorted="true"` (default), input **must** be sorted by `groupKeyFields` (group boundaries are detected by key change). Sort upstream with EXT_SORT, or set `inputSorted="false"` to use the internal hash (no sort required, more memory).

## CTL2 contract

Five entry-point functions. **Exact signatures — `transform()` has NO trailing boolean parameter:**

```ctl
function void    initGroup(Acc acc)
function boolean  updateGroup(Acc acc)
function boolean  finishGroup(Acc acc)
function integer  updateTransform(integer counter, Acc acc)
function integer  transform(integer counter, Acc acc)
```

**Call order / lifecycle:**
- `initGroup` — once per group (first record).
- `updateGroup` — once per input record in the group. Return `true` to invoke `updateTransform` for that record; `false` to skip it (typical — accumulate, emit at `finishGroup`).
- `updateTransform(counter, acc)` — runs only after an `updateGroup` that returned `true`; use to emit one record per input record.
- `finishGroup` — once after the group's input is consumed. Return `true` to invoke `transform`.
- `transform(counter, acc)` — runs after `finishGroup` returns `true`; `counter` is the call's ordinal within the group (0, 1, 2 …). Build the per-group output here.

Optional: `init()`, `preExecute()`, `postExecute()`, plus `*OnError` functions.

**Port model:** one input (`$in.0`), one or more outputs (`$out.N`), and one accumulator argument. Write `$out` only in `updateTransform` / `transform`, never in `updateGroup`.

**Idioms / keep in mind:**
- Initialize **every** accumulator field in `initGroup` — null arithmetic throws.
- `$in.0` inside `transform` reflects the **last** record of the group and is read-only there; capture earlier records into the accumulator during `updateGroup`.
- Guard divide-by-zero in `finishGroup` when computing averages.
- **`updateTransform` / `transform` are output-generation loops, not single-shot callbacks.** CloverDX calls them repeatedly with `counter = 0, 1, 2, …`; returning `ALL` (or a port number) **emits the row and continues** the loop — **only `SKIP` ends it**. (`counter` is the ordinal of *generated output rows*, not an input-row index.)
- For one-row-per-group output, guard with `if (counter > 0) return SKIP;` — an unconditional `return ALL` here loops forever.

**Minimal skeleton:**
```ctl
//#CTL2
function void initGroup(OrderAcc acc) {
    acc.count = 0;
    acc.totalFreight = 0.0d;
}
function boolean updateGroup(OrderAcc acc) {
    acc.count = acc.count + 1;
    acc.totalFreight = acc.totalFreight + $in.0.freight;
    return false;                       // emit at finishGroup, not per record
}
function integer updateTransform(integer counter, OrderAcc acc) {
    return SKIP;                        // not used here
}
function boolean finishGroup(OrderAcc acc) {
    return true;
}
function integer transform(integer counter, OrderAcc acc) {
    $out.0.customer_id   = $in.0.customer_id;
    $out.0.order_count   = acc.count;
    $out.0.total_freight = acc.totalFreight;
    return ALL;
}
```

## Examples

### Minimal — count + sum per customer

```xml
<Metadata id="META_ACC">
  <Record name="OrderAcc" type="delimited">
    <Field name="count"        type="integer"/>
    <Field name="totalFreight" type="decimal"/>
  </Record>
</Metadata>

<Node id="ROLLUP0" type="ROLLUP"
      groupKeyFields="customer_id"
      groupAccumulatorMetadataId="META_ACC"
      inputSorted="true"
      guiX="500" guiY="200">
  <attr name="transform"><![CDATA[//#CTL2
function void initGroup(OrderAcc acc) {
    acc.count = 0;
    acc.totalFreight = 0.0d;
}
function boolean updateGroup(OrderAcc acc) {
    acc.count = acc.count + 1;
    acc.totalFreight = acc.totalFreight + $in.0.freight;
    return false;
}
function integer updateTransform(integer counter, OrderAcc acc) {
    return SKIP;
}
function boolean finishGroup(OrderAcc acc) {
    return true;
}
function integer transform(integer counter, OrderAcc acc) {
    $out.0.customer_id   = $in.0.customer_id;
    $out.0.order_count   = acc.count;
    $out.0.total_freight = acc.totalFreight;
    return ALL;
}
]]></attr>
</Node>
```

### Detail + summary — one row per input record plus a group total

When `updateGroup` returns `true`, `updateTransform` fires for that record — emit a DETAIL row there; `finishGroup` returning `true` then triggers `transform` for the group SUMMARY. The `if (counter > 0) return SKIP;` guard in both caps each at one row (drop it and they loop forever). Both row types go to port 0, distinguished by a `record_type` field, so the output metadata is the superset of both shapes.

```xml
<Metadata id="META_DETAIL_ACC">
  <Record name="DetailAcc" type="delimited">
    <Field name="count"      type="integer"/>
    <Field name="total"      type="decimal"/>
    <Field name="cur_amount" type="decimal"/>
    <Field name="avg"        type="decimal"/>
  </Record>
</Metadata>

<Node id="ROLLUP_DETAIL" type="ROLLUP"
      groupKeyFields="customer_id"
      groupAccumulatorMetadataId="META_DETAIL_ACC"
      inputSorted="true"
      guiX="500" guiY="350">
  <attr name="transform"><![CDATA[//#CTL2
function void initGroup(DetailAcc acc) {
    acc.count = 0;
    acc.total = 0.0d;
    acc.cur_amount = 0.0d;
}
function boolean updateGroup(DetailAcc acc) {
    acc.count = acc.count + 1;
    acc.cur_amount = $in.0.amount;
    acc.total = acc.total + $in.0.amount;
    return true;                          // fire updateTransform for this record
}
function integer updateTransform(integer counter, DetailAcc acc) {
    if (counter > 0) return SKIP;         // one DETAIL row per input record
    $out.0.record_type = "DETAIL";
    $out.0.amount = acc.cur_amount;
    return ALL;
}
function boolean finishGroup(DetailAcc acc) {
    acc.avg = acc.count == 0 ? 0.0d : acc.total / acc.count;
    return true;                          // fire transform for the group summary
}
function integer transform(integer counter, DetailAcc acc) {
    if (counter > 0) return SKIP;         // one SUMMARY row per group
    $out.0.record_type = "SUMMARY";
    $out.0.count = acc.count;
    $out.0.total = acc.total;
    $out.0.avg   = acc.avg;
    return ALL;
}
]]></attr>
</Node>
```

## Common mistakes

- **`inputSorted="true"` (default) with unsorted input.** Group boundaries are detected by key change. Always sort by `groupKeyFields` upstream OR set `inputSorted="false"`.
- **Forgetting `groupAccumulatorMetadataId` metadata when accumulating state.** Without it, `VoidMetadata` is used and nothing can be stored across `updateGroup` calls.
- **Returning `true` from `updateGroup` when not using `updateTransform`.** Causes an unwanted call. Default to `false`.
- **Unconditional `return ALL` in `updateTransform` / `transform`.** They are output *loops* — `ALL` emits and CloverDX re-calls with `counter`+1; only `SKIP` stops them, so an unconditional `ALL` produces infinite output. Emit on the counters you need, then `return SKIP`.
- **Mutating `$in.0` in `transform`.** It reflects the last record of the group and is read-only there — capture earlier records into the accumulator.
- **Adding a trailing boolean to `transform`.** The signature is `transform(integer counter, Acc acc)` — no `boolean last` parameter.

